Dear Roksana and the VoxelMatters team,

thank you for the detailed Solidon3D article you published on 22 August:
https://www.voxelmatters.com/solidon3d-turns-a-downloaded-stl-into-a-part-that-fits-without-cad/

You already covered the four workflows, operation history, print checks,
offline use, demo terms and pricing. Rather than repeat that introduction, here
is only what has genuinely changed since the state you described — chiefly in
0.3.1.

The headline is one step beyond your article: at the time, the diameter of a
recognised hole could be changed. Now a recognised feature can be moved,
rotated, resized, duplicated and removed — a hole, a peg, a countersink or a
dome — and it keeps its identity through the edit, so dependent fits keep
their reference.

The concrete additions:

* a *Feature* panel opens at the first click on a feature and shows the
  measured values as editable fields, with a live preview while typing;
* one option applies an edit to all features of the same kind, so a row of four
  holes is one transaction with one undo;
* two selected features report their centre-to-centre distance, and a hole
  states its standard size in plain language;
* recognition is bounded by manufacturability: features below half a millimetre
  are gone, which removed 296 of 1130 entries on a downloaded hose holder;
* the default view navigation was replaced (left pans, right orbits, W/A/S/D
  flies), with the previous slicer and CAD schemes still selectable.

Version 0.3.1 alone documents 86 curated user-facing changes. The complete
history since the first 0.1 demo now contains 464.

Changelog: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html
Download: https://solidon3d.de/en/

If a short update is useful, I can provide new images and one sample project
showing a row of holes moved and resized in a single step.

